[{:db/id 17592186152087 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-25efa028-94e4-52c8-9fb8-df2dc5b08098" "question-303b12dc-4682-5eea-846a-0e1ea50fc6ae"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-25efa028-94e4-52c8-9fb8-df2dc5b08098" :question/id #uuid "25efa028-94e4-52c8-9fb8-df2dc5b08098" :question/math-academy-id "q-183509" :question/problem "$\\,$ $P(y):$ $y<y+1⇒{y}^{-1}\\in Q.$\n\nGiven that the above predicate is defined over the universal set ${Z}^{-},$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- The truth set of $P(y)$ is $∅.$\n- The truth set of $P(y)$ is ${Z}^{-}.$\n- $∀y\\in {Z}^{-},\\,P(y).$" :question/worked-solution "The *universal quantification* of a predicate $P(x),$ defined over the universal set $U,$ transforms this predicate into a statement that we write as\n\n$$\n∀x\\in U,\\,P(x).\n$$\n\nThis statement is true if the predicate $P(x)$ is true on the entire universal set $U,$ i.e. if the truth set ${T}_{P}$ of the predicate coincides with the universal set $U:$\n\n$$\n{T}_{P}=U\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that:\n\n- the truth set of \"$y<y+1$\" is ${Z}^{-},$ and\n\n- the truth set of \"${y}^{-1}\\in Q$\" is ${Z}^{-}.$\n\nSince $A⇒B≡¬A∨B,$ the truth set of our predicate is \n\n$$\n{T}_{P}=\\overset{―}{{Z}^{-}}\\cup {Z}^{-}=∅\\cup {Z}^{-}={Z}^{-}.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each of the given statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. The truth set is *not* empty.\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, ${T}_{P}={Z}^{-}.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since ${T}_{P}$ coincides with the universal set, the statement $∀y\\in {Z}^{-},\\,P(y)$ is true.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-8d365a0d-0df0-5d17-b62a-1cd26b68c01b"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-8d365a0d-0df0-5d17-b62a-1cd26b68c01b" :answer-field/id #uuid "8d365a0d-0df0-5d17-b62a-1cd26b68c01b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-ce0ec875-5a2b-517c-a707-3581499b69c7" :answer-field/choices ["answer-84e1b5bc-354e-5ae0-8448-f44b3ca5b554" "answer-ce0ec875-5a2b-517c-a707-3581499b69c7" "answer-327c8c8e-43d3-56fa-bb06-eb3e173dcf46" "answer-a51db10d-82d0-586d-9dc5-b7f7a52b5801" "answer-6d34e765-6636-5a3e-9d1c-5838b8df6736"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-84e1b5bc-354e-5ae0-8448-f44b3ca5b554" :answer/id #uuid "84e1b5bc-354e-5ae0-8448-f44b3ca5b554" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ce0ec875-5a2b-517c-a707-3581499b69c7" :answer/id #uuid "ce0ec875-5a2b-517c-a707-3581499b69c7" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-327c8c8e-43d3-56fa-bb06-eb3e173dcf46" :answer/id #uuid "327c8c8e-43d3-56fa-bb06-eb3e173dcf46" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a51db10d-82d0-586d-9dc5-b7f7a52b5801" :answer/id #uuid "a51db10d-82d0-586d-9dc5-b7f7a52b5801" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6d34e765-6636-5a3e-9d1c-5838b8df6736" :answer/id #uuid "6d34e765-6636-5a3e-9d1c-5838b8df6736" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "None of the statements are true." :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186152088 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-4c6bc667-fec4-55ad-9a19-ce10aa56b016"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-4c6bc667-fec4-55ad-9a19-ce10aa56b016" :question/id #uuid "4c6bc667-fec4-55ad-9a19-ce10aa56b016" :question/math-academy-id "q-183517" :question/problem "$\\,$ $K(y):$ $y$ is prime\n\nGiven the predicate above, which of the following expresses the statement $∃y\\in Q,\\,K(y)?$" :question/worked-solution "The existential quantifier \"$∃$\" means \"there exists.\" So, the statement \n$∃y\\in Q,\\,K(y)$ \nreads as follows:\n\n$\\,$ **There exists** $y\\in Q$ **such that** $y$ is prime.\n\n**Note:** This statement is true." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-afb31b48-45dd-5d1d-bcf6-b59e8b41fafe"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-afb31b48-45dd-5d1d-bcf6-b59e8b41fafe" :answer-field/id #uuid "afb31b48-45dd-5d1d-bcf6-b59e8b41fafe" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-945c3424-1cff-57b2-9472-12cdecf1701c" :answer-field/choices ["answer-1e4c46df-0280-55f0-ab7d-60c928de713c" "answer-c9d1dbbf-3579-5af2-80d8-490559725897" "answer-cb5dfd88-d11e-5abc-a681-95fc1039e544" "answer-945c3424-1cff-57b2-9472-12cdecf1701c" "answer-03eab922-5a85-5026-a3d7-30ed995fcbfb"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1e4c46df-0280-55f0-ab7d-60c928de713c" :answer/id #uuid "1e4c46df-0280-55f0-ab7d-60c928de713c" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "Every prime number $y$ is rational." :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c9d1dbbf-3579-5af2-80d8-490559725897" :answer/id #uuid "c9d1dbbf-3579-5af2-80d8-490559725897" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "For all $y\\in Q,$ $y$ is prime." :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-cb5dfd88-d11e-5abc-a681-95fc1039e544" :answer/id #uuid "cb5dfd88-d11e-5abc-a681-95fc1039e544" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "There exists $y\\in Q$ such that $y$ is not prime." :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-945c3424-1cff-57b2-9472-12cdecf1701c" :answer/id #uuid "945c3424-1cff-57b2-9472-12cdecf1701c" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "There exists $y\\in Q$ such that $y$ is prime." :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-03eab922-5a85-5026-a3d7-30ed995fcbfb" :answer/id #uuid "03eab922-5a85-5026-a3d7-30ed995fcbfb" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "There exists $y\\notin Q$ such that $y$ is prime." :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186152089 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-9ab7419f-dd45-5016-a048-0ef13ef60278" "question-5404de7a-e750-5076-ba3f-b3642f799e6f"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-9ab7419f-dd45-5016-a048-0ef13ef60278" :question/id #uuid "9ab7419f-dd45-5016-a048-0ef13ef60278" :question/math-academy-id "q-183552" :question/problem "$\\,$ $P(n):$ ${n}^{2}\\in N$ and $n+5>1$ \n\nGiven that the above predicate is defined over the universal set $N,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- The truth set of $P(n)$ is $∅.$\n- The truth set of $P(n)$ is $N.$\n- $∃n\\in N,\\,P(n).$" :question/worked-solution "The *existential quantification* of a predicate $P(x),$ defined over the universal set $U,$ transforms this predicate into a statement that we write as\n\n$$\n∃x\\in U,\\,P(x).\n$$\n\nThis statement is true if the predicate $P(x)$ is true for a single element $x\\in U,$ i.e. if the truth set ${T}_{P}$ of the predicate is not empty:\n\n$$\n{T}_{P}\\ne ∅\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that:\n\n- the truth set of \"${n}^{2}\\in N$\" is the set $N,$ and\n\n- the truth set of \"$n+5>1$\" is $N.$\n\nSo, the truth set of our predicate is\n\n$$\n{T}_{P}=N\\cap N=N.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each of the given statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. The truth set is *not* empty.\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, ${T}_{P}=N.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since ${T}_{P}$ is *not* empty, the statement $∃n\\in N,\\,P(n)$ is true.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-7f97a28b-3f8b-5723-9948-a37f37c51db9"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-7f97a28b-3f8b-5723-9948-a37f37c51db9" :answer-field/id #uuid "7f97a28b-3f8b-5723-9948-a37f37c51db9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-e752ccd6-f76a-546c-833f-e724e076bf9f" :answer-field/choices ["answer-45813537-dd63-5755-9b5d-ac39d95f30f1" "answer-e752ccd6-f76a-546c-833f-e724e076bf9f" "answer-b45c7085-6af1-58af-82bc-fd751002e4cf" "answer-cf8c0bae-8d50-5107-896c-3794d38f0b7e" "answer-7a78fc40-89ca-5529-8635-b81c30e2e488"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-45813537-dd63-5755-9b5d-ac39d95f30f1" :answer/id #uuid "45813537-dd63-5755-9b5d-ac39d95f30f1" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e752ccd6-f76a-546c-833f-e724e076bf9f" :answer/id #uuid "e752ccd6-f76a-546c-833f-e724e076bf9f" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b45c7085-6af1-58af-82bc-fd751002e4cf" :answer/id #uuid "b45c7085-6af1-58af-82bc-fd751002e4cf" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-cf8c0bae-8d50-5107-896c-3794d38f0b7e" :answer/id #uuid "cf8c0bae-8d50-5107-896c-3794d38f0b7e" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7a78fc40-89ca-5529-8635-b81c30e2e488" :answer/id #uuid "7a78fc40-89ca-5529-8635-b81c30e2e488" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "None of the statements are true." :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-303b12dc-4682-5eea-846a-0e1ea50fc6ae" :question/id #uuid "303b12dc-4682-5eea-846a-0e1ea50fc6ae" :question/math-academy-id "q-216465" :question/problem "$\\,$ $P(m):$ $\\frac{m}{2}$ is an integer\n\nGiven that the above predicate is defined over the universal set $Z,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- The truth set of $P(m)$ is $∅.$\n- The truth set of $P(m)$ is ${2k:k\\in Z}.$\n- $∀m\\in Z,\\,P(m).$" :question/worked-solution "The *universal quantification* of a predicate $P(x),$ defined over the universal set $U,$ transforms this predicate into a statement that we write as\n\n$$\n∀x\\in U,\\,P(x).\n$$\n\nThis statement is true if the predicate $P(x)$ is true on the entire universal set $U,$ i.e. if the truth set ${T}_{P}$ of the predicate coincides with the universal set $U:$\n\n$$\n{T}_{P}=U\n$$\n\nSince $\\frac{m}{2}$ is an integer if and only if $m$ is even, we have that the truth set of our predicate is ${T}_{P}={2k:k\\in Z}.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each of the given statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. The truth set is *not* empty.\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, ${T}_{P}={2k:k\\in Z}.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since ${T}_{P}$ doesn't coincide with the universal set, the statement $∀m\\in Z,\\,P(m)$ is false.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-7b33442d-c9c0-5d34-8c05-2ee2c223153a"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-7b33442d-c9c0-5d34-8c05-2ee2c223153a" :answer-field/id #uuid "7b33442d-c9c0-5d34-8c05-2ee2c223153a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-e1b87858-b44a-5014-877f-9dc575b84c4f" :answer-field/choices ["answer-424368b9-436c-5519-8a3e-d05dcde6b1f4" "answer-339444a3-4357-5fdf-8924-442d097e39f0" "answer-e1b87858-b44a-5014-877f-9dc575b84c4f" "answer-2d500fe4-57b9-52b5-83ea-9bda860eab27" "answer-3588a127-8777-5e98-bb9b-b646349772e5"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-424368b9-436c-5519-8a3e-d05dcde6b1f4" :answer/id #uuid "424368b9-436c-5519-8a3e-d05dcde6b1f4" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-339444a3-4357-5fdf-8924-442d097e39f0" :answer/id #uuid "339444a3-4357-5fdf-8924-442d097e39f0" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e1b87858-b44a-5014-877f-9dc575b84c4f" :answer/id #uuid "e1b87858-b44a-5014-877f-9dc575b84c4f" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2d500fe4-57b9-52b5-83ea-9bda860eab27" :answer/id #uuid "2d500fe4-57b9-52b5-83ea-9bda860eab27" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3588a127-8777-5e98-bb9b-b646349772e5" :answer/id #uuid "3588a127-8777-5e98-bb9b-b646349772e5" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "None of the statements are true." :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-5404de7a-e750-5076-ba3f-b3642f799e6f" :question/id #uuid "5404de7a-e750-5076-ba3f-b3642f799e6f" :question/math-academy-id "q-216495" :question/problem "$\\,$ $P(n):$ $n$ is odd or $n+1$ is odd\n\nGiven that the above predicate is defined over the universal set $N,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- The truth set of $P(n)$ is $∅.$\n- The truth set of $P(n)$ is $N.$\n- $∃n\\in N,\\,P(n).$" :question/worked-solution "The *existential quantification* of a predicate $P(x),$ defined over the universal set $U,$ transforms this predicate into a statement that we write as\n\n$$\n∃x\\in U,\\,P(x).\n$$\n\nThis statement is true if the predicate $P(x)$ is true for a single element $x\\in U,$ i.e. if the truth set ${T}_{P}$ of the predicate is not empty:\n\n$$\n{T}_{P}\\ne ∅\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that:\n\n- the truth set of \"$n$ is odd\" is the set of odd natural numbers, and\n\n- the truth set of \"$n+1$ is odd\" is the set of even natural numbers.\n\nSo, the truth set of our predicate is\n\n$$\n{T}_{P}={\\text{odd natural numbers}}\\cup {\\text{even natural numbers}}=N.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each of the given statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. The truth set is *not* empty.\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, ${T}_{P}=N.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since ${T}_{P}$ is *not* empty, the statement $∃n\\in N,\\,P(n)$ is true.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-589eb3e5-2666-5827-8cea-4a656b6bf3b3"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-589eb3e5-2666-5827-8cea-4a656b6bf3b3" :answer-field/id #uuid "589eb3e5-2666-5827-8cea-4a656b6bf3b3" :answer-